Key points from this Discussion:
dissociate oculomotor execution 
(main-sequence kinematics, an index of saccade-generator integrity) 
from oculomotor policy (when and where gaze is deployed), 
and characterize how policy parameters, 
and the magnitude and temporal structure of their variability, 
change across development



Eye vs. head contribution to gaze shifts
	•	Gaze shifts averaged about 15.8° from the eye alone, 9.4° from the head alone, and 23.3° combined. The eye contributed more in 83% of saccades. Head-fixed saccades were smaller, about 12.3°
	•	Mice made 1–2 gaze shifts per second during exploration
	•	idea: track the head/gaze ratio per shift and the gaze-shift rate vs. age. This is where locomotor development should show up: if head capacity grows, the head’s share could grow.

Saccade-triggered profiles in comparison to mice. 
	•	Saccade-aligned averages showed slower counter-movements of the eye just before and just after each saccade in freely moving mice; head-fixed mice showed none.
	•	The phases: eye counter-rotation as the head starts moving, then the saccade, then compensation roughly equal and opposite to the head. The pattern held across ~44,000 gaze shifts, implying the head drives gaze shifts.
	•	Asymmetry: temporal-to-nasal saccades were larger (in mice?). Nasal-to-temporal saccades brought the eye back to center; temporal-to-nasal ones carried it past center in the direction of the turn.
	•	idea: measure the latency from head-velocity onset to saccade trigger, the amplitude and duration of the pre-saccadic counter-rotation, and the asymmetry vs. age.
